ELF64 — Minimal parsing for the SLIX loader
===========================================

The SLIX loader needs to know exactly three things to bring a
binary into life: where the program wants to be (entry +
LOAD-segment addresses), how big each segment is in memory
(`p_memsz` ≥ `p_filesz` — the difference is `.bss`), and what
protection bits go on each. Everything else in the ELF spec
(symbol tables, dynamic relocations, hash tables, debug
sections) is the dynamic linker's problem; the kernel-side
loader stops at PT_LOAD.

This module is **pure parsing**. It reads `ELF64` headers and
the program-header table out of an in-memory buffer and exposes
field accessors. No I/O, no allocation, no kernel imports — so
it can be reused by anything from a debug syscall (Phase 1
chunk 7's selftest) to PM's cross-PTBR loader (this chunk's
caller) to `execve` (chunk 8) without dragging in a dependency
chain.

The buffer layout follows the ELF64 specification (System V ABI
gABI 4.1 §5). All multi-byte fields are little-endian; only
ELFDATA2LSB is supported (we'd reject big-endian binaries at
`elf64_validate`). The accessors take a buffer pointer plus a
PHT entry index when applicable; they don't keep state, which
keeps them callable across page boundaries and from any
context.

    module oskit.lib

ELF identification bytes. `EI_MAG[0..3]` is the magic; we check
all four bytes rather than relying on a single u32 read, since
the magic is byte-oriented in the spec.

    val ELF_MAG0: byte = 0x7F
    val ELF_MAG1: byte = byte('E')
    val ELF_MAG2: byte = byte('L')
    val ELF_MAG3: byte = byte('F')

EI_CLASS = 2 (ELFCLASS64) and EI_DATA = 1 (ELFDATA2LSB) are the
only combinations this loader accepts. SLIX is x86_64 and
aarch64 only; both are 64-bit and little-endian.

    val ELF_CLASS64:  byte = 2
    val ELF_DATA2LSB: byte = 1

Program-header types. We only consume `LOAD`; `INTERP` matters
for chunk 9's dynamic linker handoff; `TLS` matters for Phase 2.
The rest are listed for completeness of self-documentation, not
because the loader does anything with them.

    val ELF_PT_NULL    = 0
    val ELF_PT_LOAD    = 1
    val ELF_PT_DYNAMIC = 2
    val ELF_PT_INTERP  = 3
    val ELF_PT_NOTE    = 4
    val ELF_PT_PHDR    = 6
    val ELF_PT_TLS     = 7

Program-header flags. The bit layout matches POSIX `PROT_*`
exactly (R=4, W=2, X=1) — but with **different bit numbers**
than `VMA_PROT_*` (R=1, W=2, X=4). `elf64_pf_to_vma_prot` does
the per-bit translation.

    val ELF_PF_X = 1
    val ELF_PF_W = 2
    val ELF_PF_R = 4

`elf64_validate` return codes. 0 = ok; negatives identify which
check failed so the caller can distinguish "not an ELF binary"
(fall through to TRB) from "ELF but malformed" (fail the load).

    val ELF_OK            = 0
    val ELF_ERR_TRUNCATED = -1
    val ELF_ERR_NOT_ELF   = -2
    val ELF_ERR_NOT_64    = -3
    val ELF_ERR_NOT_LE    = -4
    val ELF_ERR_BAD_PHT   = -5

Layout sizes. ELF64 fixes both at standard values — anything
else suggests a malformed (or non-stock-toolchain) binary and
we reject.

    val ELF64_EHDR_SIZE = 64
    val ELF64_PHDR_SIZE = 56

Field offsets within `Elf64_Ehdr`. We don't expose accessors for
fields we don't yet consume (e_type, e_machine, e_flags, e_ehsize,
e_shoff/shnum/shstrndx); they're listed with offsets here as
documentation of the layout this module assumes.

    val EHDR_OFF_TYPE      = 16
    val EHDR_OFF_MACHINE   = 18
    val EHDR_OFF_VERSION   = 20
    val EHDR_OFF_ENTRY     = 24
    val EHDR_OFF_PHOFF     = 32
    val EHDR_OFF_SHOFF     = 40
    val EHDR_OFF_FLAGS     = 48
    val EHDR_OFF_EHSIZE    = 52
    val EHDR_OFF_PHENTSIZE = 54
    val EHDR_OFF_PHNUM     = 56
    val EHDR_OFF_SHENTSIZE = 58
    val EHDR_OFF_SHNUM     = 60
    val EHDR_OFF_SHSTRNDX  = 62

Field offsets within `Elf64_Phdr`. Note the layout is *not* the
same as Elf32_Phdr — `p_flags` precedes the address fields in
ELF64 (vs. trailing in ELF32). The order here matches §5.2 of
the System V ABI.

    val PHDR_OFF_TYPE   = 0
    val PHDR_OFF_FLAGS  = 4
    val PHDR_OFF_OFFSET = 8
    val PHDR_OFF_VADDR  = 16
    val PHDR_OFF_PADDR  = 24
    val PHDR_OFF_FILESZ = 32
    val PHDR_OFF_MEMSZ  = 40
    val PHDR_OFF_ALIGN  = 48

Little-endian byte readers. These are the only place this
module touches byte-order assumptions; everything above the
accessors works in host byte order. Returns are `i64` so the
caller can sign-check unsigned 64-bit values without an extra
conversion at every comparison.

    elf_read_u16_le(p: *byte) -> int
        (int(p[0]) & 0xFF) | ((int(p[1]) & 0xFF) << 8)

    elf_read_u32_le(p: *byte) -> i64
        i64(p[0] & 0xFF) | (i64(p[1] & 0xFF) << 8) | (i64(p[2] & 0xFF) << 16) | (i64(p[3] & 0xFF) << 24)

    elf_read_u64_le(p: *byte) -> i64
        i64(p[0] & 0xFF) | (i64(p[1] & 0xFF) << 8) | (i64(p[2] & 0xFF) << 16) | (i64(p[3] & 0xFF) << 24) | (i64(p[4] & 0xFF) << 32) | (i64(p[5] & 0xFF) << 40) | (i64(p[6] & 0xFF) << 48) | (i64(p[7] & 0xFF) << 56)

`elf64_validate` is the gatekeeper: it returns `ELF_OK` only
when the buffer is a well-formed ELF64 LSB image whose program
header table fits inside the buffer. Callers should run this
once before invoking any field accessor — the accessors trust
their inputs and do not bounds-check.

The PHT bound check is `phoff + phnum * phentsize <= buflen`,
done in `i64` to avoid 32-bit overflow when a malformed header
claims a huge PHT.

    elf64_validate(buf: *byte, buflen: int) -> int
        if buflen < ELF64_EHDR_SIZE
            return ELF_ERR_TRUNCATED
        if buf[0] != ELF_MAG0
            return ELF_ERR_NOT_ELF
        if buf[1] != ELF_MAG1
            return ELF_ERR_NOT_ELF
        if buf[2] != ELF_MAG2
            return ELF_ERR_NOT_ELF
        if buf[3] != ELF_MAG3
            return ELF_ERR_NOT_ELF
        if buf[4] != ELF_CLASS64
            return ELF_ERR_NOT_64
        if buf[5] != ELF_DATA2LSB
            return ELF_ERR_NOT_LE
        val phoff = elf64_phoff(buf)
        val phentsize = i64(elf64_phentsize(buf))
        val phnum = i64(elf64_phnum(buf))
        if phoff < i64(0)
            return ELF_ERR_BAD_PHT
        if phnum > i64(0)
            if phentsize < i64(ELF64_PHDR_SIZE)
                return ELF_ERR_BAD_PHT
            if phoff + phnum * phentsize > i64(buflen)
                return ELF_ERR_BAD_PHT
        ELF_OK

Header-field accessors. Caller is expected to have run
`elf64_validate` first; these don't re-check magic.

    elf64_entry(buf: *byte) -> u64
        u64(elf_read_u64_le(buf + EHDR_OFF_ENTRY))

    elf64_phoff(buf: *byte) -> i64
        elf_read_u64_le(buf + EHDR_OFF_PHOFF)

    elf64_phentsize(buf: *byte) -> int
        elf_read_u16_le(buf + EHDR_OFF_PHENTSIZE)

    elf64_phnum(buf: *byte) -> int
        elf_read_u16_le(buf + EHDR_OFF_PHNUM)

`elf64_phdr_at` returns a pointer to the i-th program-header
entry. Used by every PHT-field accessor below; pulled out so the
arithmetic is computed once per access. The caller must have
already validated `i < elf64_phnum(buf)`.

    elf64_phdr_at(buf: *byte, i: int) -> *byte
        buf + int(elf64_phoff(buf)) + i * elf64_phentsize(buf)

PHT-entry field accessors. Names mirror the spec field names so
a reader who knows ELF can find the right offset by pattern.

    elf64_ph_type(buf: *byte, i: int) -> int
        int(elf_read_u32_le(elf64_phdr_at(buf, i) + PHDR_OFF_TYPE))

    elf64_ph_flags(buf: *byte, i: int) -> int
        int(elf_read_u32_le(elf64_phdr_at(buf, i) + PHDR_OFF_FLAGS))

    elf64_ph_offset(buf: *byte, i: int) -> i64
        elf_read_u64_le(elf64_phdr_at(buf, i) + PHDR_OFF_OFFSET)

    elf64_ph_vaddr(buf: *byte, i: int) -> u64
        u64(elf_read_u64_le(elf64_phdr_at(buf, i) + PHDR_OFF_VADDR))

    elf64_ph_filesz(buf: *byte, i: int) -> i64
        elf_read_u64_le(elf64_phdr_at(buf, i) + PHDR_OFF_FILESZ)

    elf64_ph_memsz(buf: *byte, i: int) -> i64
        elf_read_u64_le(elf64_phdr_at(buf, i) + PHDR_OFF_MEMSZ)

    elf64_ph_align(buf: *byte, i: int) -> i64
        elf_read_u64_le(elf64_phdr_at(buf, i) + PHDR_OFF_ALIGN)

Translate ELF p_flags (X=1, W=2, R=4) to VMA_PROT_* (R=1, W=2,
X=4). The numeric coincidence on W=2 hides the fact that the X
and R bits *do* swap; accidentally aliasing the field would
give text segments PROT_NONE and bss PROT_X. The bit-by-bit
translation here is the only correct conversion.

    elf64_pf_to_vma_prot(pf: int) -> int
        var p = 0
        if (pf & ELF_PF_R) != 0
            p = p | 1
        if (pf & ELF_PF_W) != 0
            p = p | 2
        if (pf & ELF_PF_X) != 0
            p = p | 4
        p

Page alignment helpers used by the loader to derive VMA bounds
from an ELF segment. `vstart` is `align_down(p_vaddr)` and `vend`
is `align_up(p_vaddr + p_memsz)`. We accept the page size as a
parameter rather than importing `oskit.config.PAGE_SIZE` so this
module stays dependency-free; the caller already knows its arch's
page size.

    elf64_align_down(addr: u64, page_size: int) -> u64
        addr & ~u64(i64(page_size) - i64(1))

    elf64_align_up(addr: u64, page_size: int) -> u64
        val mask = u64(i64(page_size) - i64(1))
        (addr + mask) & ~mask
